        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Pavers do not need sealing. They are engineered to work unsealed, no manufacturer requires it, and a properly laid patio will outlast you without a drop of it. Sealing is a choice about appearance, stain resistance and joint stability, and it is a perfectly good choice when one of those matters to you.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The part to weigh honestly is that it is recurring rather than one-off. Every two to five years, for the life of the patio, with an occasional strip when the film builds up or degrades. If a driveway with oil drips, a grill area, porous stone, or simply wanting the wet look makes that worthwhile, seal it. If none of those apply and you are happy with how it looks, leaving it alone is not neglect.
          </p>
